22
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:30,960
13 is one of my favorite numbers, so I am going to use this episode to indulge myself.

23
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:41,100
As part of Unix-Kurio Number 8, that's Hacker Public Radio Episode 4657, I went on a little

24
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:52,180
flight of fancy and suggested that the calm utility could be used in place of cat.

25
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:58,420
further reflection, I realize that there are quite a few utilities that can do that job,

26
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:09,080
almost. This episode is the result of me investigating how many possible ways there are to replace

27
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:17,560
that functionality with standard utilities. Imagine that some prankster has deleted the cat utility

28
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:27,190
from your machine and you need a way to restore that capability without reinstalling it.

29
00:03:27,190 --> 00:03:33,910
I should warn you that as opposed to the rest of the series, this episode will not focus heavily

30
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:44,850
on obscure tools and it is unlikely the examples given will be very useful. Also, I am not going

31
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:49,550
to dive into the history of any of these commands.

32
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:55,730
However, you might still learn something, and it could be a little entertaining to hear

33
00:03:55,730 --> 00:04:03,140
about the ways some utilities can be twisted to this purpose.

34
00:04:03,140 --> 00:04:08,700
Let's start by reviewing what the functionality of CAT is.

35
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:16,240
Under POSIX, all it does is read the data provided on standard input, or from each file named

36
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:21,360
as an argument and writes it to standard output.

37
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:27,950
Briefly, it concatenates all of the entries.

38
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:31,070
The only option required by the standard is

39
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:37,390
hyphenU, which causes cat to write data without any delay.

40
00:04:37,390 --> 00:04:44,560
If that option were not given, the program could choose to buffer the data.

41
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:51,480
While many implementations offer additional options that modify the output, I am going to

42
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:58,260
completely ignore those and just focus on that one job.

43
00:04:58,260 --> 00:04:59,980
The file's named as arguments...

44
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
are processed in the order in which they are named.

45
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
One or more of these can be a hyphen, in which case,

46
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Kat will use the data provided on standard input,

47
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,470
and then continue to the next file in the argument list.

48
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:23,470
I will actually narrow things even further

49
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:28,470
and allow discussion of cases where only one file is involved,

50
00:05:28,470 --> 00:05:37,710
or where cat is reading from standard input via redirection from a file.

51
00:05:37,710 --> 00:05:42,710
I will start off with the too long didn't read conclusion.

52
00:05:42,710 --> 00:05:50,710
None of the standard utilities are able to fully duplicate its functionality on their own.

53
00:05:50,710 --> 00:05:56,710
Some of them come close but can't quite be relied on in all respects.

54
00:05:56,710 --> 00:06:08,010
One important reason for this lies with the POSIX definition of a text file.

55
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:16,250
This is a file treated as being separated into lines divided by new line characters.

56
00:06:16,250 --> 00:06:19,730
There are three limitations.

57
00:06:19,730 --> 00:06:26,170
None of the lines can contain a null byte, value zero.

58
00:06:26,170 --> 00:06:32,530
The lines cannot exceed the maximum line length defined by the system, required to be

59
00:06:32,530 --> 00:06:40,830
at least 4,096 bytes, including the trailing new line, and the file must end with a new

60
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:43,990
line character.

61
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:53,170
The last rule is implied by the definition of line, which states a new line must appear

62
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:56,490
at the end of a line.

63
00:06:56,490 --> 00:07:03,970
might seem strange, but a text file may contain non-asky data.

64
00:07:03,970 --> 00:07:12,360
The only restriction on the values of bytes is that they cannot be null.

65
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:20,240
Many of the standard utilities are only required to operate on text files, and therefore cannot

66
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:29,370
necessarily be relied on to handle arbitrary data. While a particular implementation might

67
00:07:29,370 --> 00:07:39,650
not have these limitations, there is no guarantee of that. I should note that both cut and paste

68
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:47,010
are only required by POSIX to operate on text files, but with the exception that the lines

69
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:57,280
can be of any length. In addition to Cat, the only utilities covered in this episode

70
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:07,820
that are required by POSIX to handle any arbitrary file are DD tail when used with the

71
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:18,820
hyphen c option, that's c as in Charlie, t, that's spelled t e e or in the phonetic alphabet,

72
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:27,820
tango echo echo, t r and u u encode.

73
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:35,900
The wording for the compress utility does not specifically say it must handle any file,

74
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:39,700
But in practice I think this is true.

75
00:08:39,700 --> 00:08:45,800
However, all of these utilities have at least one limitation of their own.

76
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:53,980
They cannot handle more than one file, or they can only operate on standard input.

77
00:08:53,980 --> 00:09:01,580
Some other utilities are able to deal with multiple files, standard input, or both, but

78
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:07,300
those are only required to operate on text files.

79
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:14,620
In practice, this typically means that if an input file does not end with a new line character,

80
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:22,700
the program will add one to the output, destroying the IIDEA that the output of cat should be identical

81
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:26,740
to the input.

82
00:09:26,740 --> 00:09:32,180
Let's start with the least capable options and work our way towards those that could

83
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:40,420
serve as closer drop-in replacements. The first candidate is a utility that I thought might

84
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:50,660
be persuaded to work, but it doesn't quite. This is NL, a command used to number lines of

85
00:09:50,660 --> 00:10:00,060
files or standard input. It normally prints the contents of this input, and on the left

86
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:09,810
left prints the number of each line. It is possible to turn off the numbering and to set

87
00:10:09,810 --> 00:10:18,530
the separator between the numbers and the text to a null string. However, it does not

88
00:10:18,530 --> 00:10:27,130
seem possible to have absolutely nothing up here in front of each line. The POSIX specification

89
00:10:27,130 --> 00:10:34,410
states that the hyphen w option can be given to define how many characters to the left

90
00:10:34,410 --> 00:10:39,450
of each line will be reserved for the line numbers.

91
00:10:39,450 --> 00:10:48,970
While the default value is 6, it says nothing about what values must be accepted.

92
00:10:48,970 --> 00:11:00,050
I tested several implementations, specifically from Genu, Free BSD, Net BSD, and Open Indiana,

93
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:07,050
which is an open source derivative of System 5, and none of them allow setting the width

94
00:11:07,050 --> 00:11:13,010
of the space for line numbers to be less than 1.

95
00:11:13,010 --> 00:11:21,330
Even if it were possible to remove the preceding space entirely, POSIX only requires NL to accept

96
00:11:21,330 --> 00:11:30,010
one file argument and to operate on text files, so it would still be limited.

97
00:11:30,010 --> 00:11:38,120
You can find the actual commands to use in the show notes for this episode.

98
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:45,600
Next up are methods that actually work, but are only usable with standard input.

99
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:52,560
When combined with input redirection from a file, they can accept a single file and send

100
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:56,330
it to standard output.

101
00:11:56,330 --> 00:12:05,160
The read utility takes a line of input and assigns it to a shell variable.

102
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:08,340
It requires a little help, however.

103
00:12:08,340 --> 00:12:15,600
It can't output what it reads, so it has to be paired with something like Echo or Print

104
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:25,720
F. Because it reads input one line at a time, it needs to be used in a loop to read the entirety

105
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:28,640
of the data.

106
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:37,020
In addition, the IFS shell variable must be set to a null string to prevent read from

107
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:39,660
doing field splitting.

108
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:45,080
We don't want it to modify the data being read at all.

109
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:50,300
There is a shell loop you can find in the show notes that will do the job, except that

110
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:55,500
read is only required to handle text files.

111
00:12:55,500 --> 00:13:01,300
It is not clear to me whether POSIX allows the shell to have a limit on how much data can

112
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:03,780
be stuffed into a variable.

113
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:11,060
If it does, that could be a problem, even if your implementation of read can handle lines

114
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:21,220
longer than the maximum length. The TR utility is another one that only works with standard input.

115
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:29,940
It takes the input, replaces any characters in the first string argument with the corresponding

116
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:39,260
characters in the second string argument, and sends the result to standard output. If the first

117
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:51,340
and second strings are the same, such as t-r-a-a less than symbol my file, it will behave like cat.

118
00:13:54,550 --> 00:14:02,870
The t command also fits into this category. It copies standard input to standard output,

119
00:14:02,870 --> 00:14:10,550
just like cat does. It also sends a copy to any file name arguments given.

120
00:14:10,550 --> 00:14:19,810
If none of these are present, it just passes the data through untouched.

121
00:14:19,810 --> 00:14:28,210
You could use t less than symbol my file to print a file.

122
00:14:28,210 --> 00:14:34,630
Be careful, however, not to enter your file name as an argument to t, as that will cause

123
00:14:34,630 --> 00:14:38,160
it to be overwritten.

124
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:48,750
The good news with both TR and T is that they aren't limited to working with text files.

125
00:14:48,750 --> 00:14:56,470
Let's now consider a couple of options that only work on a file, not with standard input.

126
00:14:56,470 --> 00:14:59,950
In addition, they can only handle one single.

127
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:07,040
file. This might seem to defeat the purpose of cat, which can combine multiple files,

128
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:18,450
but cat is often used with only one file. These are ed and ex to text editors.

129
00:15:20,370 --> 00:15:26,530
By feeding them their right command sequence on standard input, they can be convinced to

130
00:15:26,530 --> 00:15:35,410
output the entire file and then exit. The appropriate invocations are available in the show notes.

131
00:15:37,410 --> 00:15:45,010
The POSIX specification for EX indicates that more than one file name can be given as an argument.

132
00:15:47,010 --> 00:15:53,730
I attempted to take advantage of this with a command line that would feed a set of commands

133
00:15:53,730 --> 00:16:01,110
over and over to EX, which should print the first file named as an argument to standard

134
00:16:01,110 --> 00:16:07,150
output, and then move to the next one, and print that.

135
00:16:07,150 --> 00:16:14,870
In my reading of the specification, once the last file has been printed, the next command

136
00:16:14,870 --> 00:16:21,800
should result in an error, causing the program to exit.

137
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:29,840
However, the implementations that I tested don't exit and just stay on the last file, printing

138
00:16:29,840 --> 00:16:33,080
it out endlessly.

139
00:16:33,080 --> 00:16:42,240
Perhaps I'm misunderstanding how EX works, but regardless, both it and Ed are only required

140
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:52,670
to handle text files, so they can't completely serve in place of Cat anyway.

141
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:58,990
The following category of replacements is capable of using either standard input or a

142
00:16:58,990 --> 00:17:04,510
file, but they can only work with one file.

143
00:17:04,510 --> 00:17:10,510
Let's start with COM which was responsible for me exploring this question in the first

144
00:17:10,510 --> 00:17:13,160
place.

145
00:17:13,160 --> 00:17:25,400
command com my file slash dev slash null will send my file to standard output. If you want

146
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:32,160
to use standard input instead of a file, you need to replace the first file name with a

147
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:42,310
hyphen. The paste utility works similarly. It can only be used with one file because if

148
00:17:42,310 --> 00:17:49,510
If more are given, it will try to interleave the lines of these files together.

149
00:17:49,510 --> 00:17:53,750
Another possibility is tail.

150
00:17:53,750 --> 00:18:03,350
When used with the hyphen c plus one option, it outputs the entirety of the file.

151
00:18:03,350 --> 00:18:10,710
Unlike the other implementations I tried, the net bsd version of tail is capable of taking

152
00:18:10,710 --> 00:18:14,310
multiple file names as arguments.

153
00:18:14,310 --> 00:18:21,190
However, in that case, it precedes the content of each file with the file name,

154
00:18:21,190 --> 00:18:27,430
like head does, so it doesn't produce a clean copy.

155
00:18:27,430 --> 00:18:35,110
The sort utility used with the hyphenm option, which I talked about in

156
00:18:35,110 --> 00:18:45,990
Unix-Curio number 11, that's HackerPublicRadio episode 4687, can also do the job.

157
00:18:45,990 --> 00:18:54,070
All of these are only required to handle text files by POSIX, except for TALE, which

158
00:18:54,070 --> 00:19:03,440
must handle any arbitrary file when the hyphen C option is used.

159
00:19:03,440 --> 00:19:11,200
One more utility that can take either one file or standard input is DD.

160
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:15,640
It is a bit different from these others in a couple ways.

161
00:19:15,640 --> 00:19:22,120
First, it writes statistics about the data processed to standard error.

162
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:29,720
So you would need to redirect that to slash dev slash null to replicate the behavior of

163
00:19:29,720 --> 00:19:32,200
cat.

164
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:37,760
To use standard input, no arguments are needed.

165
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:45,720
The syntax that DD uses for arguments is unusual compared to other Unix utilities.

166
00:19:45,720 --> 00:19:56,280
For this purpose, DDIF equals myFile to greater than symbol slash dev slash null would be the

167
00:19:56,280 --> 00:19:58,280
command to use.

168
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:09,000
The Compress and UU encode utilities are in the same category, but do a lot more processing on the data along the way.

169
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:18,440
The purpose of these is described respectively in Unix Curios number 7 and number 1.

170
00:20:18,440 --> 00:20:28,720
Their output can be piped into a corresponding uncompressed or UUD code utility to get back

171
00:20:28,720 --> 00:20:32,920
the original data and send it to standard output.

172
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:38,280
See the show notes for the specific commands to use.

173
00:20:38,280 --> 00:20:47,400
I should point out that when I tested the UU encode method on free BSD 15.0, it didn't

174
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:48,400
work.

175
00:20:48,400 --> 00:21:01,610
However, it did work on NetBSD 10.1 and OpenIndiana 2025.10.

176
00:21:01,610 --> 00:21:07,170
Next we have two possibilities that come very close, but don't quite behave exactly like

177
00:21:07,170 --> 00:21:09,620
cat.

178
00:21:09,620 --> 00:21:16,300
They send to standard output either the files named as arguments in order or, if there are

179
00:21:16,300 --> 00:21:20,000
none, standard input.

180
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:26,080
However, POSIX does not require them to take a hyphen as an argument representing standard

181
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:28,950
input.

182
00:21:28,950 --> 00:21:36,150
First off, we have said, when called with an empty script, it just prints out the contents

183
00:21:36,150 --> 00:21:40,070
without any transformations.

184
00:21:40,070 --> 00:21:43,830
The grep utility works the same way.

185
00:21:43,830 --> 00:21:50,870
When given an empty regular expression, it treats that as matching anything and prints

186
00:21:50,870 --> 00:21:56,080
each line of input to standard output.

187
00:21:56,080 --> 00:22:02,880
Finally, we have arrived at the utilities that can fully replace CAT, albeit only for text

188
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:05,280
files.

189
00:22:05,280 --> 00:22:12,080
They can be given one or more files, including a hyphen representing standard input, and will

190
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:17,040
output them in the order they are given as arguments.

191
00:22:17,040 --> 00:22:24,910
If no file name arguments are given, they just use standard input instead.

192
00:22:24,910 --> 00:22:28,830
Starting out, we have AUK.

193
00:22:28,830 --> 00:22:34,910
While an empty pattern matches every line of input, and an empty action prints the entire

194
00:22:34,910 --> 00:22:43,210
line, POSIX states that if both the pattern AND the action are empty, the utility is to

195
00:22:43,210 --> 00:22:47,310
simply exit without reading or writing anything.

196
00:22:47,310 --> 00:22:54,630
Therefore, we need to at least provide a pattern or an action.

197
00:22:54,630 --> 00:22:58,870
A pattern can be shorter.

198
00:22:58,870 --> 00:23:04,590
Another one that has the capabilities is cut.

199
00:23:04,590 --> 00:23:11,510
However, as noted earlier, these will add a new line character if the input doesn't

200
00:23:11,510 --> 00:23:18,660
already end with one, so they aren't exact replacements.

201
00:23:18,660 --> 00:23:24,420
I was hoping that a simple invocation of one of the standard utilities could be used in

202
00:23:24,420 --> 00:23:29,140
place of the CAT program that our prankster has deleted.

203
00:23:29,140 --> 00:23:34,980
However, I think I've demonstrated that isn't possible.

204
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:42,980
Let's return to those utilities that can handle arbitrary files, but can't take more than one at a time.

205
00:23:42,980 --> 00:23:49,980
It would be possible to write a shell script to manage any options and arguments,

206
00:23:49,980 --> 00:23:56,980
then use the utility to do the actual work of sending through the data file by file.

207
00:23:56,980 --> 00:24:13,290
In my view, tail would be the best fit because it can take a bare file name as an argument and, unlike DD, uses the standard syntax for Unix utilities.

208
00:24:13,290 --> 00:24:21,560
I have provided a script in the show notes that does just that.

209
00:24:21,560 --> 00:24:29,560
Unfortunately, it is not something simple that could be typed in from memory.

210
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:37,200
So, probably it would be easier in most situations to just reinstall cat on your system instead

211
00:24:37,200 --> 00:24:46,030
of looking up this script, typing it in, and saving it where cat used to be.

212
00:24:46,030 --> 00:24:52,370
It might be tempting to implement this substitute as an alias or shell function rather than

213
00:24:52,370 --> 00:24:59,130
as a script, but those only exist in the current shell execution environment and its

214
00:24:59,130 --> 00:25:00,130
some shells.

215
00:25:00,460 --> 00:25:06,460
and are not inherited by utilities and scripts run by you or others.

216
00:25:06,460 --> 00:25:14,030
So the easiest way to get it to be used by other scripts, such as cron jobs,

217
00:25:14,030 --> 00:25:20,030
would be to save a copy of it on your system where cat normally lives,

218
00:25:20,030 --> 00:25:27,270
making sure that the Execute File Mode bits are set.

219
00:25:27,270 --> 00:25:34,270
There are certainly other widespread tools, such as Perl, that could also be used in place of CAD.

220
00:25:35,570 --> 00:25:44,370
However, I wanted to focus only on utilities that are so ubiquitous that they appear in the POSIX standard.

221
00:25:45,670 --> 00:25:55,070
It should be noted that even some of these, such as Compress and UU encode, aren't always present on modern systems.

